      "provenance": "literature_retrieved",
      "rationale": "[Literature audit 2026-11-05 (deliverome-analysis PR 236), verdict confirmed_shifted: insert_after_residue 98 -> 99 on human canonical Q9BRK3 (paper numbering: ortholog:mouse, construct species: mouse). Paper (PMID 32075743): \"The mouse Mxra8 + moo was generated by inserting residues GEQRVGEQRLGEQRV after amino acid residue 98.\". Audit note: Full text via PMC7163869. Mouse Mxra8 (Q9DBV4) residue 98 is V in ..YSAGEQRV|YEPRDR..; human has one extra N-terminal residue so the same V is human 99 (..YSAGEQR V|YEARDR..): human insert_after should be 99 (V|Y), not 98 (R|V). Insertion is a natural bovine loop, not an epitope; surface detected by flow; it abolishes CHIKV infection (function perturbed by design).] L1 explicitly names residue 98 of mouse Mxra8 as the insertion point for the 15-aa moo tag in a full-length surface-displayed construct. Python confirms that human MXRA8 residue 98 = R and residue 99 = V, both within the extracellular domain (landmarks: EC 20\u2013341), 244 residues from the TM boundary \u2014 well clear of the \u22653-residue constraint. Because the citation is for the mouse ortholog, position_evidence is 'inferred' and cited_tag_residue is set to 98 (the mouse residue carrying the published tag). L1 (PMC7163869): A 15-residue 'moo' sequence was inserted after residue 98 of mouse Mxra8 in a full-length, membrane-anchored construct used to study surface expression. Mouse Mxra8 is a close ortholog of human MXRA8; the insertion site is mapped to human residue 98 by sequence correspondence.",
